You have been tasked with creating a program that manages a list of appointments for a busy doctor's office. The program must allow for multiple users to access and modify the appointment list simultaneously, while also ensuring that appointments are not double-booked and that no race conditions occur. 
To accomplish this, you decide to use channels in Go to allow for concurrent access to the appointment list. However, you also need to ensure thread-safety and prevent race conditions.
To do this, you create a mutex that locks and unlocks access to the appointment list. Whenever a user wants to access or modify the list, they must first acquire the lock by calling the mutex.Lock() function. Once they are done, they must release the lock by calling mutex.Unlock().
Here's a sample code snippet that demonstrates how to properly use the mutex to manage concurrent access to shared data:
```
type Appointment struct {
    PatientName string
    Date string
    Time string
}
type AppointmentList struct {
    appointments []Appointment
    mutex sync.Mutex
}
func (al *AppointmentList) AddAppointment(appt Appointment) {
    al.mutex.Lock()
    defer al.mutex.Unlock()
    // Check if appointment already exists
    for _, a := range al.appointments {
        if a.Date == appt.Date && a.Time == appt.Time {
            return
        }
    }
    // Add new appointment to list
    al.appointments = append(al.appointments, appt)
}
func (al *AppointmentList) GetAppointments() []Appointment {
    al.mutex.Lock()
    defer al.mutex.Unlock()
    return al.appointments
}
```
In this example, the AppointmentList struct contains a slice of appointments and a mutex. The AddAppointment function adds a new appointment to the list, while the GetAppointments function returns the list of appointments.
To ensure thread-safety and prevent race conditions, both functions first acquire the lock by calling al.mutex.Lock(), then release it by calling defer al.mutex.Unlock(). This ensures that only one user can access or modify the appointment list at a time, preventing any conflicts or race conditions from occurring.
Overall, by using a mutex to manage concurrent access to shared data, you can ensure that your appointment management program is thread-safe and free from race conditions, even when multiple users are accessing it simultaneously.

