To calculate the average rating for a movie across various genres in C++, you can use an array to store the ratings and then calculate the average by summing up all the ratings and dividing by the number of genres. Here's some sample code:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    string genres[] = {"Drama", "Action", "Adventure", "Fantasy", "Thriller", "Animation", "Documentary"};
    int ratings[] = {4, 3, 5, 4, 3, 4, 4};
    int numGenres = sizeof(genres) / sizeof(genres[0]);

    int totalRating = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < numGenres; i++) {
        totalRating += ratings[i];
        cout << genres[i] << "\t" << ratings[i] << endl;
    }

    double avgRating = (double) totalRating / numGenres;
    cout << "Average rating: " << avgRating << endl;

    int highestRating = -1;
    int lowestRating = 6;
    string highestGenre, lowestGenre;
    for (int i = 0; i < numGenres; i++) {
        if (ratings[i] > highestRating) {
            highestRating = ratings[i];
            highestGenre = genres[i];
        }
        if (ratings[i] < lowestRating) {
            lowestRating = ratings[i];
            lowestGenre = genres[i];
        }
    }

    cout << "Highest rated genre: " << highestGenre << " (" << highestRating << ")" << endl;
    cout << "Lowest rated genre: " << lowestGenre << " (" << lowestRating << ")" << endl;

    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first define the genres and ratings as arrays. We then calculate the total rating by looping through the ratings array and summing up the values. We also print out the genres and ratings in a table format.
Next, we calculate the average rating by dividing the total rating by the number of genres. We then loop through the ratings array again to find the highest and lowest rated genres. We keep track of the highest and lowest ratings as well as their corresponding genres.
Finally, we print out the highest and lowest rated genres along with their ratings.
