To calculate the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a matrix using MATLAB, you can use the built-in function `eig()`. Here is the MATLAB code implementation for the given matrix:
```
A = [1 2 3; 4 5 6; 7 8 9];
[eigenvectors, eigenvalues] = eig(A)
```
This will output the eigenvectors and eigenvalues of matrix A.
To visualize the eigenvectors as arrows in a 3D plot, you can use the `quiver3()` function in MATLAB. Here is the MATLAB code implementation for this visualization:
```
% Calculate eigenvectors and eigenvalues
A = [1 2 3; 4 5 6; 7 8 9];
[eigenvectors, eigenvalues] = eig(A);
% Plot eigenvectors as arrows in a 3D plot
figure;
quiver3(zeros(3,1), zeros(3,1), zeros(3,1), eigenvectors(1,:), eigenvectors(2,:), eigenvectors(3,:));
xlim([-1 1]);
ylim([-1 1]);
zlim([-1 1]);
xlabel('x');
ylabel('y');
zlabel('z');
title('Eigenvectors as Arrows');
grid on;
```
This will create a 3D plot with the eigenvectors represented as arrows. The `quiver3()` function takes in the x, y, and z coordinates of the starting point of each arrow (in this case, all starting at the origin), and the x, y, and z components of each eigenvector. The `xlim()`, `ylim()`, and `zlim()` functions set the limits of the x, y, and z axes, respectively. The `xlabel()`, `ylabel()`, and `zlabel()` functions label the axes, and the `title()` function sets the title of the plot. The `grid on` command adds a grid to the plot for better visualization.

