```
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.Set;
public class UniqueAcronymGenerator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String sentence = "What are the potential limitations of quantum computing?";
        String[] words = sentence.split(" ");
        Set<String> acronyms = new HashSet<>();
        Random random = new Random();
        while (true) {
            StringBuilder acronymBuilder = new StringBuilder();
            for (String word : words) {
                acronymBuilder.append(word.charAt(0));
            }
            String acronym = acronymBuilder.toString();
            if (!acronyms.contains(acronym)) {
                System.out.println("Unique and catchy acronym: " + acronym);
                break;
            }
            words = shuffle(words, random);
        }
    }
    private static String[] shuffle(String[] words, Random random) {
        for (int i = words.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
            int j = random.nextInt(i + 1);
            String temp = words[i];
            words[i] = words[j];
            words[j] = temp;
        }
        return words;
    }
}
```
This code first splits the sentence into an array of words. It then generates random permutations of the words until a unique acronym is found. The shuffle() method is used to randomly shuffle the words in the array. The HashSet is used to keep track of the acronyms that have already been generated to ensure that the acronym generated is unique.
When you run the code, it will output a unique and catchy acronym that can be used within the quantum computing community.

