```
def longestCommonPalindrome(str1: String, str2: String): String = {
  val n = str1.length
  val m = str2.length
  val table = Array.ofDim[Int](n+1, m+1)
  var maxLength = 0
  var endIndex = 0
  for (i <- 1 to n) {
    for (j <- 1 to m) {
      if (str1(i-1) == str2(j-1)) {
        table(i)(j) = table(i-1)(j-1) + 1
        if (table(i)(j) > maxLength && isPalindrome(str1.substring(i-table(i)(j), i))) {
          maxLength = table(i)(j)
          endIndex = i
        }
      }
    }
  }
  str1.substring(endIndex-maxLength, endIndex)
}
def isPalindrome(str: String): Boolean = {
  str == str.reverse
}
val str1 = "ABACDFG"
val str2 = "BCDFGAH"
println(longestCommonPalindrome(str1, str2))
```
This code creates a two-dimensional table with dimensions (n+1)x(m+1), where n and m are the lengths of the two input strings. The table is used to store the length of the longest common substring at each position (i,j) in the two input strings. The algorithm iterates through each position in the table and fills it in by comparing the characters at that position in the two input strings.
If the characters match, the length of the longest common substring is increased by 1 and the algorithm checks if the current substring is a palindrome. If it is longer than the current longest palindrome, it updates the maxLength and endIndex variables.
Finally, the function returns the longest common palindrome substring by using the endIndex and maxLength variables to extract the substring from the first input string. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n*m), where n and m are the lengths of the two input strings.

