There are several techniques in C++ that can be implemented to improve the performance of a computer. Here are two examples:
1. Loop Unrolling: 
Loop unrolling is a technique in which the compiler generates multiple copies of the loop body and executes them sequentially. This can reduce the overhead of loop control instructions and improve performance. Here is an example of loop unrolling:
```
for (int i = 0; i < N; i += 4) {
  // Unroll the loop by 4 iterations
  result[i] = a[i] + b[i];
  result[i+1] = a[i+1] + b[i+1];
  result[i+2] = a[i+2] + b[i+2];
  result[i+3] = a[i+3] + b[i+3];
}
```
In this example, the loop is unrolled by four iterations. This means that each iteration of the loop will execute four statements instead of one. This can reduce the overhead of loop control instructions and improve performance.
2. Cache Optimization:
Cache optimization is a technique in which the data is arranged in memory to take advantage of the cache hierarchy. This can reduce the number of cache misses and improve performance. Here is an example of cache optimization:
```
const int CACHE_LINE_SIZE = 64;
const int N = 1024;
// Allocate arrays with padding to avoid false sharing
int a[N + CACHE_LINE_SIZE / sizeof(int)];
int b[N + CACHE_LINE_SIZE / sizeof(int)];
int result[N + CACHE_LINE_SIZE / sizeof(int)];
// Initialize the arrays
for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
  a[i] = i;
  b[i] = i;
  result[i] = 0;
}
// Compute the sum of the arrays
for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
  result[i] = a[i] + b[i];
}
```
In this example, the arrays are allocated with padding to avoid false sharing. False sharing occurs when two or more threads access different variables that are located on the same cache line. This can cause unnecessary cache invalidations and reduce performance. By allocating the arrays with padding, we can ensure that each variable is located on a separate cache line, which can reduce false sharing and improve performance.

