Here is an example implementation of the stream-of-consciousness technique in Swift using a timer and a text view:
```swift
import UIKit
class ViewController: UIViewController {

    @IBOutlet weak var textView: UITextView!

    let phrases = ["Echoes in the abyss", "Lost in the darkness", "A flicker of light", "Memories fade away", "The void consumes me"]
    var currentIndex = 0

    var timer: Timer?

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()

        // Start the timer to update the text view every 2 seconds
        timer = Timer.scheduledTimer(timeInterval: 2.0, target: self, selector: #selector(updateTextView), userInfo: nil, repeats: true)
    }

    @objc func updateTextView() {
        // Randomize the order of phrases
        let shuffledPhrases = phrases.shuffled()

        // Get the next phrase in the shuffled array
        let nextPhrase = shuffledPhrases[currentIndex]

        // Update the text view with the next phrase
        textView.text = "\(textView.text ?? "")\n\(nextPhrase)"

        // Increment the index or reset to 0 if at the end of the array
        currentIndex = (currentIndex + 1) % shuffledPhrases.count
    }
}
```
This implementation uses a timer to periodically update the text view with a random phrase from an array. The `shuffled()` method is used to randomize the order of phrases, and the `currentIndex` variable is used to keep track of which phrase to display next. The `updateTextView()` method is called every 2 seconds by the timer, and it appends the next phrase to the text view.
