Here's a Java code solution that generates all possible permutations of the friends' powers and calculates the result for each combination. It then returns the combination of powers that results in the number needed to take down the forcefield:
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
public class FriendsPowers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 100; // the number needed to take down the forcefield
        String[] powers = {"A", "B", "C", "D", "E"}; // the friends' powers
        ArrayList<String[]> permutations = new ArrayList<String[]>();
        generatePermutations(powers, permutations, 0);
        for (String[] permutation : permutations) {
            int result = calculateResult(permutation, x);
            if (result == x) {
                System.out.println(Arrays.toString(permutation));
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    public static void generatePermutations(String[] powers, ArrayList<String[]> permutations, int index) {
        if (index == powers.length) {
            permutations.add(powers.clone());
        } else {
            for (int i = index; i < powers.length; i++) {
                swap(powers, index, i);
                generatePermutations(powers, permutations, index + 1);
                swap(powers, index, i);
            }
        }
    }

    public static void swap(String[] powers, int i, int j) {
        String temp = powers[i];
        powers[i] = powers[j];
        powers[j] = temp;
    }

    public static int calculateResult(String[] powers, int x) {
        int result = x;
        for (String power : powers) {
            switch (power) {
                case "A":
                    result *= 3;
                    break;
                case "B":
                    result /= 2;
                    break;
                case "C":
                    result += 10;
                    break;
                case "D":
                    result -= 5;
                    break;
                case "E":
                    result *= result;
                    break;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```
In this code, we first define the number needed to take down the forcefield and the friends' powers. We then generate all possible permutations of the friends' powers using a recursive function called `generatePermutations`. This function swaps the elements of the `powers` array to create all possible permutations and adds each permutation to an `ArrayList` called `permutations`.
Next, we loop through each permutation in `permutations` and calculate the result using the `calculateResult` function. This function takes in the permutation and the number needed to take down the forcefield, and applies each friend's power to the number in sequence. The final result is returned.
If the result of a permutation is equal to the number needed to take down the forcefield, we print out the permutation and break out of the loop.
Note that this code assumes that there is only one combination of powers that can take down the forcefield. If there are multiple combinations that work, this code will only return the first one it finds.

