How can I simulate a scenario in which the financial loss from holding onto an asset outweighs the potential gain in MATLAB?
One possible way to do this is by using the Monte Carlo simulation technique. First, we can define a probability distribution for the potential gains and losses of the asset over a certain period of time. Then, we can simulate multiple scenarios by drawing random samples from these distributions and calculating the net present value (NPV) of each scenario. Finally, we can compare the distribution of NPVs to determine the probability of experiencing a financial loss that outweighs the potential gain.
Here's an example MATLAB code that demonstrates this approach:
% Define probability distributions for gains and losses
gainDist = makedist('normal', 'mu', 0.1, 'sigma', 0.05); % mean=10%, std=5%
lossDist = makedist('normal', 'mu', 0.2, 'sigma', 0.1); % mean=20%, std=10%
% Simulate multiple scenarios
nScenarios = 10000;
nYears = 5;
scenarios = zeros(nScenarios, nYears);
for i = 1:nScenarios
    gains = random(gainDist, nYears, 1);
    losses = random(lossDist, nYears, 1);
    scenarios(i, :) = cumprod(1 + gains) .* cumprod(1 - losses);
end
% Calculate NPV of each scenario
discountRate = 0.05;
cashFlows = ones(nScenarios, nYears) * 1000; % assume initial investment of $1000
npvs = zeros(nScenarios, 1);
for i = 1:nScenarios
    npvs(i) = sum(cashFlows(i, :) ./ (1 + discountRate).^ (1:nYears)) ...
        - sum(losses);
end
% Plot distribution of NPVs
histogram(npvs, 'Normalization', 'probability');
xlabel('Net Present Value ($)');
ylabel('Probability');
title('Distribution of NPVs for Asset Holding Scenario');

