Here's one way you could modify your code to remove all vowels and consecutive consonants recursively:
```
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "unicode"
)
func main() {
    input := "Hello World"
    output := removeVowels(input)
    fmt.Println(output)
}
func removeVowels(input string) string {
    runes := []rune(input)
    output := make([]rune, 0, len(runes))
    for i := 0; i < len(runes); i++ {
        if !unicode.IsLetter(runes[i]) {
            continue
        }
        if unicode.In(runes[i], unicode.Latin) && !isVowel(runes[i]) {
            output = append(output, runes[i])
        }
        if len(output) > 1 && !isVowel(output[len(output)-1]) && !isVowel(output[len(output)-2]) && output[len(output)-1] == output[len(output)-2] {
            output = output[:len(output)-1]
        }
    }
    return string(output)
}
func isVowel(r rune) bool {
    switch unicode.ToLower(r) {
    case 'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u':
        return true
    default:
        return false
    }
}
```
This code uses the `unicode` package to check if a character is a letter or a vowel. It also keeps track of the output string as a slice of runes, since strings in Go are immutable.
The `removeVowels` function loops through the input string, ignoring any non-letter characters. For each letter, it checks if it is a vowel using the `isVowel` function. If it is not a vowel, it is added to the output slice.
After adding a letter to the output slice, the function checks if the last two letters in the slice are both consonants. If they are and they are the same letter, the last letter is removed from the output slice to prevent consecutive consonants.
The final output is returned as a string.
